    {
      "id": "news-penn-ai-scribe",
      "slug": "penn-in-house-ai-scribe",
      "kind": "news",
      "title": "Penn's in-house AI scribe is on about 1,200 ambulatory providers",
      "dek": "The Philadelphia Inquirer, Nov. 10, 2025: internist Dina Capalongo hits a green button; the doctor still approves the note.",
      "body": "Dina Capalongo, a Penn Medicine internist, hits a green button on her phone. The draft note is usually on the screen before she leaves the room, the Philadelphia Inquirer reported Nov. 10, 2025. The product is Penn’s in-house AI scribe. Ambient listening records the visit and writes a sectioned note the doctor must approve. About 1,200 ambulatory providers were using it, the Inquirer said. A separate 46-clinician Penn pilot in JAMA Network Open (Feb. 19, 2025) associated a scribe with 10.3 minutes in notes per visit falling to 8.2. Kevin B. Johnson, who leads Penn’s AI4AI Lab, called those “early but promising results.” The journal article does not name a commercial vendor. Epic Systems’ Signal tool timed the EHR use.",
      "status": "published",
